 "public_summary": "A flat-bottomed ~780 ppm dip repeats every 5.65 days for 25 transits and implies a 2.2 Earth-radius planet around a late-K dwarf. Its shape, duration and lack of odd-even or secondary eclipses match a planet and show no signs of a binary on the target. The TESS depth does vary somewhat between sectors and the 9-arcsec neighbour can in principle mimic it with a ~10% eclipse, but the difference imaging is consistent with the target and the best explanation overall is a small transiting planet, pending follow-up that can confirm the depth on the right star.",
